\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The Porcelain Falcon\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Miriam didn\'92t mean to buy the falcon.\uc0\u8232 She went into the shop to get her watch repaired \'97 though she couldn\'92t remember if it was broken \'97 and left carrying a porcelain bird wrapped in brown paper.\
The shopkeeper had been certain she\'92d ordered it.\uc0\u8232 He\'92d even shown her the receipt, with her name scrawled in a handwriting that was definitely hers except for all the letters being upside down.\u8232 When she tried to explain that she didn\'92t need a falcon, he simply tapped the counter and said,\
\'93You\'92ll understand when the tide comes in.\'94\
The tide never came in.\uc0\u8232 The city was 200 miles from the nearest shore.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Chapter 1 \'97 The Man in the Red Chair\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Miriam kept the falcon on the mantle, where it stared at the wall instead of the room.\uc0\u8232 She thought about turning it around but didn\'92t, because the man in the red chair might notice.\
No one else believed in the man.\uc0\u8232 He wasn\'92t always there, but when he was, he sat so still that Miriam couldn\'92t tell if he was breathing.\u8232 Sometimes she\'92d leave the room for a moment and come back to find the chair empty \'97 but the indentation on the cushion deeper.\
The man never spoke, except for the time he said,\
\'93Don\'92t wind the clock unless the window is shut.\'94\
She didn\'92t own a clock.\uc0\u8232 Or a window.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Chapter 2 \'97 The Train That Didn\'92t Arrive\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 A letter arrived one morning, slid under her door without an envelope.\uc0\u8232 It was just a single sentence:\
\'93Your train leaves at noon, platform seven.\'94\
She checked her watch \'97 still not broken \'97 and saw that it was 11:45.\uc0\u8232 There was no station in her city.\u8232 Still, she found herself walking until she arrived at a row of benches facing nothing but a brick wall.\
A man in a station uniform was selling tickets for a train that, he admitted freely, had never once arrived.\uc0\u8232 Miriam bought one anyway.\
When noon came, the brick wall shimmered, and for a moment she thought she heard the low, distant hum of an engine.\uc0\u8232 Then the wall went back to being a wall.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Chapter 3 \'97 The Disappearing Cup of Tea\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 She returned home to find the falcon gone.\uc0\u8232 In its place was a steaming cup of tea.\u8232 She reached for it, and it vanished, leaving only the faint smell of lemon.\
A knock came at the door.\uc0\u8232 It was the shopkeeper, holding the falcon.\u8232 He swore he\'92d never seen it before.\
When she asked him why he\'92d taken it, he blinked as if she\'92d spoken in another language and said,\
\'93You\'92ll miss the tide if you keep asking questions.\'94\
She reminded him there was no tide.\uc0\u8232 He said, \'93That\'92s why it\'92s dangerous.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Chapter 4 \'97 The Clock That Ran on Smoke\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One morning the man in the red chair was gone, and in his place was a clock.\uc0\u8232 Its hands were made of smoke, drifting slowly but never settling on a number.\u8232 When she tried to touch them, her fingers went right through, and for hours afterward everything she touched smelled faintly of burnt sugar.\
The clock had no mechanism, no back panel, and yet she could hear it ticking \'97 not steadily, but like a nervous heart.\
That evening, she heard it stop.\uc0\u8232 The silence was heavy enough to bend the air.\u8232 She turned around, and the falcon was facing the room now.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Chapter 5 \'97 The Ending That Happens in the Middle\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 One night she dreamed of the end of the story.\uc0\u8232 She was standing on a shore (though she\'92d never left the city), the tide finally coming in, carrying dozens of porcelain falcons bobbing on the waves.\
The man in the red chair was there, but now the chair was blue.\uc0\u8232 He told her it was time to wind the clock.\u8232 When she said she still didn\'92t have one, he only smiled and pointed to the horizon.\
The sun was setting in the north.\
She woke up in Chapter 2 again, holding a ticket to a train that wouldn\'92t arrive, wondering if this was where the story had begun or where it ended.\
